2026-06-30 15:26:17,602 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-97726e21-c15e-414a-80bd-40650427bfc9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nSP_Q_87.raw\nSP_Q_88.raw\nSP_Q_89.raw\nSP_Q_90.raw\nSP_Q_91.raw\nSP_Q_92.raw\nSP_Q_93.raw\nSP_Q_94.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nEstablishment of in vivo proximity labeling with biotin using TurboID in the filamentous fungus Sordaria macrospora\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProximity-dependent biotin identification (BioID) has emerged as a powerful methodology to identify proteins co-localizing with a given bait protein in vivo. The approach has been established in animal cells, plants and yeast but not yet in filamentous fungi. BioID relies on promiscuous biotin ligases fused to bait proteins to covalently label neighboring proteins with biotin. Biotinylated proteins are specifically enriched through biotin affinity capture from denatured cell lysates and subsequently identified and quantified with liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC–MS). In contrast to many other affinity capture approaches for studying protein–protein interactions, BioID does not rely on physical protein–protein binding within native cell lysates. This feature allows the identification of protein proximities of weak or transient and dynamic nature. Here, we demonstrate the application of BioID for the fungal model organism Sordaria macrospora (Sm) using the example of the STRIPAK complex interactor 1 (SCI1) of the well-characterized striatin-interacting phosphatase and kinase (SmSTRIPAK) complex as proof of concept. For the establishment of BioID in S. macrospora, a codon-optimized TurboID biotin ligase was fused to SCI1. Biotin capture of the known SmSTRIPAK components PRO11, SmMOB3, PRO22 and SmPP2Ac1 demonstrates the successful BioID application in S. macrospora. BioID proximity labeling approaches will provide a powerful proteomics tool for fungal biologists.\n\n[METHODS]\nMethods\nAll S. macrospora strains, primers and template plasmids used for PCR amplifications as well as all plasmids generated in this study are listed in Supplementary Tables\xa0S1, S2 and S3, respectively. A detailed description of strains, media, growth conditions, transformation of S. macrospora, generation of single-spore isolates, protein extraction as well as the generation of plasmids can be found in the Supplementary Note 1.\nWestern blot and immunodetection\nFor the immunodetection of proteins, the proteins were transferred onto a Protan BA85 nitrocellulose membrane (Cytiva) using the Semi-dry Xpress Blotting kit (Serva) according to the instructor’s manual after SDS-PAGE. Blotted proteins were visualized using 0.2% Ponceau\xa0S in 3% TCA solution. After documentation, the membrane was fully destained through washing with TBST and the membrane was blocked using 5% milk powder in 1\u2009×\u2009TBST. Membranes for the analysis of biotinylated protein through Streptavidin-HRP were blocked overnight at 4\xa0°C, washed 3\u2009×\u200915\xa0min with 1×\u2009TBST, and incubated 50\xa0min with Streptavidin-HRP [Thermo Scientific (21130), 1:30,000]. Expression analysis of free TurboID and SCI1-TurboID fusion proteins was performed using an anti-HA (Sigma-Aldrich (H9658), 1:3000) or anti-BirA antibody (antibodies-online GmbH (ABIN2691501), 1:2000). Subsequently, membranes were incubated with secondary HRP-coupled antibodies (HRP-linked anti mouse, Sigma-Aldrich (A5278), 1:10,000; HRP-linked anti rabbit, Thermo Scientific (G21234), 1:5000) for 60\xa0min in darkness. For detection of a luminescent signal, the membranes were covered in 500 µL WesternBright ECL HRP (Advansta Corporation) substrate. Chemiluminescence was detected and documented using the digital Vilber Lourmat FUSION SL imaging system (PEQLAB Biotechnologie GmbH).\nBiotin affinity purification using Strep-Tactin Sepharose\nFor the BioID experiment with Δsci1::p5\'-sci1-L-TurboIDect, four independent biological replicates were cultivated in 50\xa0ml SWG (50\xa0nM biotin, 40\xa0mg/L arginine) for 4\xa0days at 27\xa0°C under continuous light conditions. Before harvest, the strains were boosted by adding 20\xa0ml SWG supplemented with 30\xa0µM biotin for 30\xa0min. Due to evaporation throughout the 4\xa0days of growth, roughly 40\xa0ml of the initial SWG medium remained in the Petri dishes. Therefore, boosting with 20\xa0ml medium results in a final biotin concentration of 10\xa0µM. The wt::pc-L-TurboIDect strain, which expresses an unfused TurboID, was used as control in this experiment. As the ccg1 overexpression promoter controls the expression of the free TurboID ligase in the control strain, this strain exhibits stronger biotinylation levels than the strain expressing the SCI1-L-TurboID fusion protein. Thus, the crude protein extract of the control was diluted 1:6 with wild-type extract (not boosted with biotin) to match the biotinylation level of the Δsci1::p5\'-sci1-L-TurboIDect strain. After protein extraction, 800\xa0µL crude protein extract were mixed with 200\xa0µL 20%\xa0SDS to obtain a final concentration of 4%\xa0SDS. The samples were denatured by heating to 65\xa0°C for 5\xa0min. Biotinylated proteins were enriched using Strep-Tactin Sepharose (IBA Lifesciences GmbH) similar to Schmitt and Valerius (2019). In brief, 100\xa0µL of slurry were equilibrated per sample by two washing steps with 375\xa0µL 1×\u2009Buffer W. Then, 1\xa0ml denatured protein extract was added to the beads and incubated on a rotating wheel for 30\xa0min at RT. Afterwards, the crude protein extract containing unbound biotinylated proteins was separated from the beads by centrifugation, and the beads were washed three times with 1×\u2009Buffer\xa0W (+\u20090.4%\xa0SDS). Next, biotinylated proteins were eluted by adding 75\xa0µL 1×\u2009Buffer BXT and 10\xa0min incubation on the shaker. The supernatant containing the biotinylated proteins was collected by centrifugation at 400×g for 2\xa0min. This elution step was performed twice for a total of 150\xa0µL eluate. Eluates for in gel digestions were entirely concentrated in the SpeedVac and resuspended in TE Buffer and 2\u2009×\u2009Tris–Glycine/SDS sample buffer. The whole eluate was loaded onto a 12%\xa0polyacrylamide gel and was run until the bromophenol of the loading dye migrated through roughly 1/3 of the resolving gel. The gels of the BioID experiments were incubated in fixing solution for 30\xa0min, and the lanes of interest were excised using a clean scalpel. Each lane was fractioned in approximately 2\xa0mm big pieces, which were stored in 1.5\xa0ml Protein LoBind tubes filled with A. dest.\nTrypsin in-gel digest and C18 STAGE tip purification\nThe tryptic in-gel digestion, C18 STAGE tip purification and LC–MS analysis were performed as previously described in Groth et al. (2021).\nAnalysis of LC–MS raw data\nLC-MS raw files were analyzed using the MaxQuant (version 1.6.10.43) and Perseus (version 1.6.0.7) software. MS raw data files were searched against the S. macrospora specific proteomics data set Smacrospora_v03_peptides.fasta from Blank-Landeshammer et al. (2019) with max missed cleavage sites = 3. Oxidation of methionine, N-terminal acetylation of proteins, Phosphorylation (STY) and biotinylation of lysines were set as variable modifications. “Trypsin/P” was used as digestion mode. Samples were quantified by label-free quantification. The decoy sequences were generated by the revert mode, and the false discovery rate (FDR) was set to 0.01. The MaxQuant output file “proteinGroups.txt” was used for further data analysis in Perseus according to the workflow listed in Supplementary Table S4.\nHomologs of the putative interaction partners were identified through the UniProtKB BLAST (available at: https://www.uniprot.org/blast/; accessed on 6th December 2021) with the “UniProtKB reference proteomes plus Swiss-Prot” database setting. Searches for homologs in S. cerevisiae were performed with the BLAST of the Saccharomyces Genome Database (SGD) (available at: https://www.yeastgenome.org/blast-sgd; accessed on 6th December 2021).\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nValidation of TurboID constructs\nFor the establishment of BioID in S. macrospora, multiple plasmids were generated and transformed into wild-type or the Δsci1 SmSTRIPAK mutant (Supplementary Note 1, Supplementary Tables S1–S3). These transformed strains express either the SCI1-TurboID fusion protein or an unfused TurboID ligase, the latter one serving as negative control (Fig.\xa01). The TurboID ligase was codon-optimized according to the codon usage table of S. macrospora (Supplementary Fig. S1). In addition, a 3xHA-tag was C-terminally fused to TurboID for convenient detection in immunoblots. Furthermore, we constructed plasmids with and without an 11 amino acid MGGGGSGGGGS linker at the N-terminus of TurboID. The expression of free TurboID is either controlled by the ccg1 constitutive promoter (pc) of the clock-controlled gene 1 from Neurospora crassa or the xylose inducible Smxyl promoter (px) of the beta-xylanase gene (SMAC_08023) from S. macrospora. Either the native sci1 promoter (p5’) or the constitutive ccg1 promoter controls expression of the SCI1-TurboID fusion protein in the Δsci1 strain.\nBased on the amino acid sequence, the expected molecular masses were calculated for free TurboID (38.5\xa0kDa), the SCI1-TurboID fusion protein (71.6\xa0kDa), and the linker (0.8\xa0kDa). To verify expression and compare the protein levels among the free TurboID and SCI1-TurboID strains, all available strains were grown in liquid Biomalt maize (BMM) medium for 3\xa0days at 27\xa0°C. After protein extraction, the crude protein extract was separated by SDS–polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) and immunoblotted using a monoclonal anti-HA antibody for signal detection (Fig.\xa02, Supplementary Fig. S2).\nThe free TurboID ligase and the SCI1-TurboID fusion proteins were detected at their expected molecular masses in the Western blot experiment (Fig.\xa02). In addition, the expression of TurboID was verified with an anti-BirA antibody (Supplementary Fig. S3).\nFor the SCI1-BioID experiments we decided to use the native sci1 promoter for authentic expression regulation to remain at the most physiological conditions for the SCI1-BioID experiments. The functionality of the SCI1-TurboID fusion protein was confirmed by complementation of a S. macrospora Δsci1 mutant. Deletion of sci1 causes sterility due to the inability to develop fruiting bodies. Hence, the transf\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSordaria macrospora\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
